<div class="article-body">

<p><strong>The short answer: hydroponics costs more to start ($150 to $300) but less over a year. Soil costs less to start ($50 to $100) but adds up fast with annual refills. I proved this by switching and saving money.</strong></p>

<p>I wanted to grow my own food. So I did what everyone does. I bought bags of potting mix, containers, fertilizer, and a bag of compost. Total: about $80. Felt good. Cheap setup.</p>

<p>But here is what nobody told me. I had to rebuy everything every season. Soil degrades. Nutrients wash out. Pots crack. By month 8, I had spent over $300 on soil gardening supplies. That is when I started doing the math.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I am not a gardening expert. I am someone who spent too much money on dirt before figuring out there is a cheaper way to grow food indoors.</p>

<h2 id="setup-cost">1. The Setup Cost Trap: Where Most People Get It Wrong</h2>

<p>Every article online says "soil is cheaper to start." That is true for the first weekend. You spend $50 on bags of potting mix, $20 on seeds, $15 on containers. You feel good about saving money.</p>

<p>But soil is not a one-time cost. It is a subscription you do not realize you signed up for.</p>

<p>Potting mix degrades in 3 to 6 months. Nutrients wash out every time you water. You have to buy fertilizer, compost, pest control, and more soil every season. By the time your third harvest rolls around, you have already spent more than a hydroponic setup would have cost.</p>

<p>Here is the hidden math most guides skip. A $50 bag of potting mix lasts about 3 to 4 months for 5 to 6 containers. That is $150 to $200 per year on soil alone. Add fertilizer, perlite, and pest treatments and you are at $300 to $400 annually for a small indoor garden.</p>

<p>I learned this the expensive way. Let me show you my actual numbers.</p>

<h2 id="soil">2. What I Actually Spent on Soil Gardening</h2>

<p>I kept receipts for my first 8 months of soil gardening. Here is what it looked like.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">My Soil Gardening Costs (8 Months)</div>
  <p>$65: Potting mix (3 bags over 8 months). $30: Fertilizer and compost. $25: Perlite and drainage materials. $20: Pest control (neem oil, diatomaceous earth). $15: Replacement pots (cracked or root-bound). $20: Seeds (multiple batches). $170 total for 8 months. That is $255 per year if I kept going.</p>
</div>

<p>And that does not include water. Soil gardens need more water because most of it evaporates or drains away. I was watering every 2 days in summer. My water bill went up about $8 per month.</p>

<p>Here is the part that frustrated me. I was not even getting great harvests. My lettuce took 50 to 60 days. My herbs were okay but not amazing. And I kept buying more stuff to fix problems instead of just growing food.</p>

<p>I thought I had it all figured out. Then a friend let me try his hydroponic lettuce and it tasted better and grew in half the time. I started questioning everything.</p>

<h2 id="hydro">3. The $150 Hydroponic Setup That Changed My Mind</h2>

<p>I was skeptical. Hydroponics sounded expensive and complicated. Pumps and nutrients and pH meters. Sounded like a science experiment, not a garden.</p>

<p>But I was already spending $30 per month on soil supplies. A basic <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=DWC+hydroponic+starter+kit&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">DWC hydroponic starter kit</a> was $60. I bought one. Then I needed a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=LED+grow+light+for+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">basic LED grow light</a> for $40. A pH meter for $15. Nutrients for $25. Total: $140.</p>

<p>That first batch of lettuce was ready in 28 days. Not 55. Twenty eight. Same seed, same room temperature, but the hydroponic system grew it twice as fast.</p>

<p>Here is the honest part. I did kill my first batch. I set the pH wrong and the lettuce turned yellow. But I fixed it. The second batch was perfect. And I harvested $30 worth of lettuce from a system that cost me $140 total.</p>

<p>After 3 months, the hydroponic system had paid for itself. I stopped buying potting mix. I stopped buying fertilizer every month. I just bought nutrient concentrate every 6 to 8 weeks for $15.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Break-even Math</div>
  <p>Soil: $255 per year. Hydroponics: $140 setup + $90 per year in nutrients and electricity. Break-even: month 5. After that, hydroponics saves about $165 per year. That is $825 over 5 years assuming you replace the pump once ($15).</p>
</div>

<h2 id="monthly">4. Monthly Cost: What You Actually Spend Each Month</h2>

<p>Let me break this down by month so you can see the difference clearly.</p>

<p><strong>Month 1.</strong> Soil: $80 (setup). Hydroponics: $140 (setup). Soil looks cheaper by $60.</p>

<p><strong>Month 3.</strong> Soil: $30 (more potting mix, fertilizer). Hydroponics: $0 (nutrients still good). Hydroponics is now cheaper.</p>

<p><strong>Month 6.</strong> Soil: $60 (soil, fertilizer, pest control). Hydroponics: $15 (new nutrients). Hydroponics saves $45 this month.</p>

<p><strong>Month 12.</strong> Soil: $255 total. Hydroponics: $140 setup + $90 consumables = $230 total.</p>

<p>By the end of year 1, hydroponics saves $25. By year 2, it saves over $200 because the setup cost is already paid for. Soil requires annual refills forever. Hydroponics requires nutrients and electricity.</p>

<p>That electricity cost? For a 40W LED strip running 16 hours a day, it is about $4 per month at average US rates. Check the <a href="https://growlightdb.tabletopharvest.com" target="_blank">Grow Light DB</a> to calculate exact electricity costs for specific lights. Most people overestimate how much a small LED costs to run. It is less than a Netflix subscription.</p>

<h2 id="hydro-wins">5. Where Hydroponics Wins the Cost Battle</h2>

<p>I tested both methods side by side for 4 months. Here is where hydroponics clearly beat soil on cost.</p>

<p><strong>Growth speed.</strong> Hydroponic lettuce is ready in 28 to 35 days. Soil takes 50 to 65 days. That means I harvest 2x more food from the same setup in the same time. More harvests per dollar spent on seeds and nutrients.</p>

<p><strong>Water use.</strong> Hydroponics uses up to 90% less water. My water bill went down by $5 to $8 per month after switching. That is $60 to $96 per year saved just on water.</p>

<p><strong>No soil to replace.</strong> This is the biggest hidden cost. I do not buy potting mix anymore. Ever. The clay pebbles in my <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroponic+clay+pebbles+expanded+clay&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">hydroton clay pebbles</a> are reusable. I rinse them between grows and they work for years. Soil cannot do that.</p>

<p><strong>Pest control.</strong> Indoor hydroponics has almost zero pests. No fungus gnats from wet soil. No mold on potting mix. I have not bought neem oil in 6 months. That saved me about $20 per season.</p>

<p>Here is the surprising part. I did not expect hydroponics to be cheaper. I expected it to be the "luxury" option. But the numbers do not lie. If you grow leafy greens and herbs indoors, hydroponics saves money within the first year.</p>

<h2 id="soil-wins">6. Where Soil Is Actually Cheaper (Honest)</h2>

<p>I am not going to pretend hydroponics wins everywhere. There are situations where soil is the better financial choice.</p>

<p><strong>Outdoor gardening.</strong> If you have a backyard and grow in the ground, soil is essentially free. You do not need potting mix. You do not need grow lights. You just need seeds, water, and time. Hydroponics cannot compete with free sunlight and free soil.</p>

<p><strong>Root vegetables.</strong> Carrots, potatoes, beets. They do not grow well in hydroponics. They need the physical resistance of soil to develop properly. If you want root vegetables, grow them in soil.</p>

<p><strong>Large fruiting plants.</strong> Tomatoes, peppers, squash. You can grow them hydroponically, but you need a big system. A 5-gallon DWC bucket per plant costs more than just planting tomatoes in a raised bed.</p>

<p><strong>If you have under $50.</strong> Hydroponics needs at least $100 to $150 for a decent starter setup. If you literally cannot spend that, soil is your only option. You can grow herbs in a $10 pot with $5 of potting mix and $3 of seeds. Something is better than nothing.</p>

<p>I want to be honest. For a small kitchen herb garden with basil and mint, soil might be simpler and cheaper if you already have pots. My recommendation is not anti-soil. It is anti-lying-about-the-true-cost-of-soil. Most guides skip the refill cost. I am not going to do that.</p>

<p>If you already have a soil garden and it works for you? Keep it. But if you are starting fresh and planning to grow leafy greens or herbs indoors, <a href="/posts/hydroponics-for-beginners">hydroponics is the cheaper option over 12 months</a>.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">7. My Verdict: When to Pick Each One</h2>

<p>Here is my rule. If you are growing indoors, in a small space, and want leafy greens or herbs: hydroponics wins on cost after 5 months. The upfront investment is real but you stop buying soil forever.</p>

<p>If you are growing outdoors, in the ground, or want root vegetables: use soil. It is the right tool for that job.</p>

<p>If you are completely broke and want to start with $30: grow in soil. Any food you grow is better than buying groceries. Upgrade to hydroponics when you have the budget.</p>

<p>If you want to save the most money long term: do both. Hydroponics indoors for greens and herbs year-round. Soil outdoors for tomatoes and root vegetables in season. That is what I do now and my grocery bill dropped from $120 per month to about $60 per month for fresh produce.</p>

